# extern_type_struct.tkr — the §16 C1 KEYSTONE fixture: `extern type Name = struct { … }`. It
# declares `Timespec` as a header-less C-ABI struct matching libc's `struct timespec`, an
# `extern fn clock_gettime(clk, ts: ref Timespec)` out-pointer call, then STORES a value fresh off
# the extern call (`ts.sec` into a plain `i64`) and reads it back. Before C1 this exact shape was a
# C COMPILE ERROR: a same-shaped Teko `struct` carried a `tk_struct_hdr __hdr` word, so the value was
# a nominally distinct 24-byte type un-storable from a libc `struct timespec *`. The header-less
# emit makes the layout byte-identical, so the value stores and reads; a positive wall-clock second
# (> 1_700_000_000) exits 0, proving the round-trip on a live system.

Feature: extern_type_struct — an `extern type = struct` value crosses the FFI boundary and is stored

  Scenario: clock_gettime_value_is_storable — a value fresh off an extern call stores and reads back
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 0
